A||||15||goldfinger||How to make my new fiero run longer?||||1||
Z||000000||goldfinger||10-30-2000||02:21 PM||||What can I do to my fiero that is free or inexpensive that will make it run longer and better. I am getting mine on thursday. It will have brand new brakes and rotors. Also I am putting a Sony CDX 5050x cd player, audiobahn 4x6 plates, 2 12inch rockford fosgate subs and a 250a fosgate amp. Please tell me what I can do. Thanks guys and the wait should be over sooon!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||olympic||10-30-2000||02:41 PM||||Synthetic engine and tranny oil is a good place to start.  Use synthetic grease on the suspension.  Make sure the suspension/steering is tight and get e 4 wheel alignment to save your tires from uneven wear.  Flush the cooling system every 2 years or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sdm1964||10-30-2000||03:12 PM||||Drive responsible, keep it clean, do regular maintenance. Keep a good attitude, because things are going to break, wear out. And they are like Lays potato chips, can't have just one!!!!!||||reg||6
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-30-2000||03:13 PM||||Sounds like the you did a lot with the radio, and brakes. The radio should run for a long time. What got replaced on the engine?  Does the car have a 200,000 mile engine in it or a 30,000 mile engine in it?<P>Synthetic lubricants are good stuff I use it in my new car, but would hesitate to use it in a high milage car.  All those detergents will really clean the engine out.<P>Good air filter to keep the dirt out of the engine. Balance the wheels and the stuff that olympic said.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||10-30-2000||03:25 PM||||Don't redline it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||03:47 PM||||goldfinger,<P>When I read your post, I read "run longer" to mean keeping the stereo playing w/o running the engine for a longer amount.  If I am totally off base ignore my post and stick to the above opinions.  If I am correct invest in an Optima "style" battery and a start guard that will not allow you to drain your battery beyond a voltage that is required to start the vehicle.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000006||JohnnyK||10-30-2000||03:58 PM||||William: I'm pretty sure your pretty far off..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anywayas, I say sell the stereo, and get an engine rebuild.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||OzFiero||10-30-2000||09:29 PM||||I think the comment on maintaining a good attitude is right on. I bought my Fiero expecting the same reliability we get from GM products in this country, WRONG! A car with 50K miles needed new suspension bushes and shocks all around, new distributor,new alternator, new heater core,TCC switch & roof lining. I love driving the car but get less enthusiastic with each failure. Better save your money unless you want to listen to that stereo while parked only.||||reg||9
Z||000008||jlruiz67||10-30-2000||09:59 PM||||My 87 Fiero GT has now over 200k miles on it and It has had a water pump replaced......that's it everything else is original and no real problems, The former original owner changed the oil every 3k miles and did all the regular maintenance with records.I've had it for the last 3 years 30k miles. I will be doing a 3.4 upgrade one of these days just because of the milage, it runns geat and I must admit I have a lead foot so I get 15 miles per galon. I really "Love" this car and if you treat it right it will treat you right.<P>------------------<BR>Life's a beach||||reg||14
Z||000009||AL||10-30-2000||10:05 PM||||i beg to differ,i've had my gt for over a year and i haven't had a problem with it at all,what i do know is it's the luck of the draw.now if i was you i would follow all service requirements,second i would put in 10 w 40 in the engine it's a little thicker but who cares,i've done this with ll my cars and i have had trouble free driving from all of them,now if the car needs something fixs it immediateley it seems that once something goes bad it starts to affect everything else,i do'nt want to bore you any more but just take care of your, car wash it every week and it will pay you back with solid dependability,good luck<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by AL (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||StuGood||10-30-2000||11:34 PM||||Wow, OzFiero! Australia-?  You must be one of the most far-out members of this Forum !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Very sorry to hear of your bad luck with Fieros  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  My Fiero experiences have been pretty much the opposite of yours.  In fact, mine have proven to be extremely reliable and forgiving vehicles.  For some reason, I haven't even had most of the "typical" Fiero problems (e.g. - electrical problems, squeaky belts, and so forth).  When they <B>do</B> break, or get wrecked, I have found them very tough and repairable.  I'd continue, but if I brag on them too much, my cars will no doubt fall apart as I type this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>I notice this is your first post, so welcome to the Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Good luck, and if you need help with anything, please don't hesitate to post your questions here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  ||||reg||10
Z||000011||Vlad||10-31-2000||02:08 AM||||By the way......<P>I have a freand of mine who lives in Moscow, Russia and owns 88 GT.<P>He's been offered 5k to sell his Fiero to filming company which was going to blow it up in the movie. <BR>He didn't accept that offer.......))||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tigger||10-31-2000||09:15 AM||||Although no conclusive studies have been reported to prove this... I still believe if you talk to your Fiero it will run better.<P>Read it a good article on a wild engine swap every once in a while.  Put a TV in the garage so your Fiero can watch shows and movies like "Back to the Future," "The Wraith," and "Gone in 60 Seconds," no wait, you don't wanna scare it.  Anyway, my Fiero likes to watch "The Dukes of Hazzard."   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Hehe..  j/k  This is too much fun.  <P>Lots of good pointers have already been given here, as far as things going bad.  They probably will like any used car, and yes, each one gets a little more fustrating.  It took me about 5 DNF's before I got all the bugs worked out of mine.  Most of those things were stuff the previous owner caused or just happened spontaneously.  Even if you do have something go wrong, likely it won't be that hard to replace and if you have questions just ask the forum.<P>Might be a good idea to change the plugs, wires, and distributer cap if they look old.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||tgowens||10-31-2000||11:00 AM||||<B>goldfinger</B>, the thing to remember is that these cars are now between 13 and 17 years old -- parts wear out under normal usage due to age -- there will be things you need to replace over time just due to age -- but, think of it this way, when you replace them all you will have a new car!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Reality is, there is good advice here - replace the common things, fluids, belts, etc  on a scheduled basis. Don't abuse the car and it will give you longer life.<P>We are replacing quite a few 2.5L and 2.8L engines here at the shop which breathes new life into the car. We use synthetic fluids which perform better and we stick to maintenance schedules. -- <P>But, my point is, expect a normal amount of squeeks and rattles of an approximately 15 year old car -- just take care of it and it <I>will</I> last longer......<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                          <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group||||reg||1
Z||000014||ROB MAC||10-31-2000||11:42 AM||||Aside from general maintenance, regular oil change and coolant change , get up in between the trunk and rear fenders and all around the battery area -CLEAN IT , PAINT IT , RUST TREAT IT !!!!!!! This is a PRIME ROT area!!!!!! Pull back the carpet in the upper side trunk and look for early signs of trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||10-31-2000||02:07 PM||||The problem with the Fiero's is they are SO deceptive because they look so good, still.  So you have a hard time remembering they are 13-17 years old because they don't look it.  Some parts are going to wear due to use and some are going to decay due to time and weather.<P>I bought an '87 Fiero GT with 64,000 miles on it.  It had been stored for 5 years until I bought it.  2 problems:  oil leak from distributor o-ring, and brake lines dry-rotted and failed.  Otherwise, it's been as reliable as a car with 64,000 miles on it.<P>To OzFiero, didn't I see you on TV at the Olympics?  Well, maybe it wasn't you, but it was SOME Australian!  Welcome to the forum.  If you want to come to the Wisconsin Dells Fiero run, I'll pick you up at the airport in Milwaukee, let you stay over at my place, and give you directions.  I don't know the best way to get your car shipped here.  St. Lawrence seaway, through the great lakes to port of Milwaukee??  Better check into it soon, though, because it's in late spring or early summer (here in northern hemisphere).||||reg||1
